SAY   By Simon Hattenstone.
PAUSE 1200
SAY   There were four silver trunks in Mary Austin's attic that she packed in the mid-1980s and hadn't opened since. It was too painful. Almost 40 years on, in 2022, she opened one. It was crammed with lyrics from songs written by Freddie Mercury – Don't Stop Me Now, Killer Queen, We Are the Champions and, of course, Bohemian Rhapsody. She shut the case. It was still too painful.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "To say I was overwhelmed is an understatement," she says. "I couldn't look at the lyrics. I felt as I did when I originally packed them – that they were his personal belongings and I was being offered an opportunity to delve into them, and I couldn't do that." Eventually she asked her two sons to sift through the treasures on her behalf.
PAUSE 500
SAY   The original handwritten lyrics belonged to her. As does Mercury's house, which she has lived in for more than 30 years. As does half of his estate, which has grown over the decades from an estimated £20-30m to around £150m. In the years since Mercury died, Austin has become extraordinarily rich, although she denies reports that her share of the estate rose to 75 percent after the death of his parents.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Many of Queen's fans have accused her of hoarding his treasures, of making a fortune from the band's legendary frontman without lifting a finger, of denying them and his family access to the most basic truths, including where his ashes were scattered. Meanwhile, Mercury appeared to regard her as the one person in the world he could wholly trust and Austin has quietly dedicated her days to him.
PAUSE 500
SAY   The relationship between Austin and Mercury is captivating. Over the years it has fascinated his fans, not least because she has so rarely talked about it publicly.
PAUSE 500
SAY   We meet at the Guardian offices in London. Austin has a beautiful face (when she smiles, she's got a touch of Debbie Harry), walks with a stick, and is elegantly but unobtrusively dressed. Everything about her is unobtrusive. Austin is here with her younger son, Jamie, who is in his mid-30s and was born a couple of months after Mercury died in 1991, aged 45. Strangely, he looks a little like the singer, although Mercury is not his father.
PAUSE 500
SAY   She is about to publish Freddie Mercury: A Life in Lyrics, a book that combines the lyrics in their original scratchy form, photographs and her observations on the relationship between both the lyrics and Mercury, and Mercury and herself. As you'd expect of somebody who has guarded his privacy fiercely since his death, it is hardly a kiss'n'tell. Nor is today's interview. But she is remarkably candid about their life together.
PAUSE 500
SAY   She and Mercury, who was born Farrokh Bulsara 80 years ago this week, were an unlikely couple. He was loud, flamboyant, confident and supremely camp. She was quiet, inhibited, diffident and conservative. He was a rock star in one of the world's most successful groups; she was a shop assistant turned manager at Biba. He loved flashing the cash, whether to indulge himself or for good causes. She didn't give a toss about material stuff. He was gay, she was strictly heterosexual.
PAUSE 500
SAY   They met in 1970, when Mercury was running a clothes and textile stall in Kensington Market in London after graduating from his graphic art and design degree. Austin was a friend of Brian May's, and one day she was at May's student house when he, Mercury and Roger Taylor were discussing possible names for their new band. May suggested Build Your Own Boat. Mercury proposed Queen.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "They asked what I thought," she says. "I said I preferred Build Your Own Boat." Did she really think that? "Oh, no. I thought it was a terrible name. But Brian was my friend. I'd just met Freddie."
PAUSE 500
SAY   What were her first impressions? "I thought he was too confident for me." She describes him standing by the fireplace, while she sat on the sofa, and I can see her transported back to the day. "He was full of bravado. I picked up a magazine and started thumbing through it because I felt I shouldn't be there. Why was I sitting in a conversation I had no part in? Yes, he was very confident and I found it a bit intimidating."
PAUSE 500
SAY   But they soon became friends and lovers, and within four months, Mercury had suggested they move in together. The idea might have been partly pragmatic, she says. He wanted to live in London and couldn't afford a place by himself. So they moved in together and split the rent.
PAUSE 500
SAY   They bonded over their difficult childhoods. Mercury was born in Zanzibar, and at seven was sent to a British boarding school near Mumbai. Over the next seven years he saw his parents only once, Austin says. He came to Britain for the first time at 17.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Austin grew up with two deaf parents who struggled for money. Her mother was a cleaner and her father a factory worker. Her mother died when Austin was 15, and she found herself bringing up her younger sisters. Both she and Mercury felt they had been robbed of their childhoods. "Freddie could see the sadness in me that he felt within himself, and vice versa."
PAUSE 500
SAY   She soon discovered he wasn't quite as sure of himself as he appeared. "It was a mix of real confidence and bravado. I think the mix was to cover and protect who he really was." Not surprisingly, Mercury was confused by his identity. He was born in Africa to Parsi Indian parents and spoke with an English plum in his mouth. At the time, brown pop stars were few and far between.
PAUSE 500
SAY   As a Queen-loving child, I had no idea he was Indian. "He hid that well," Austin says. Does she think he was passing as white English? "Yes. He'd stay out of the sun because his skin would darken, and he didn't want that. With Freddie, you just saw what he wanted you to see. He'd get stopped in the street by police and they said, where are you from, and when he said Bombay they arrested him, because they thought he was taking the proverbial."
PAUSE 500
SAY   Did he speak Indian languages? "Never in front of me. His parents would speak Gujarati, but he spoke English back."
PAUSE 500
SAY   Despite the band being called Queen and Mercury being outrageously camp, when I was young it never crossed my mind that he was gay. This was partly because he was going out with Austin, and partly because it was the glam rock era, when any self-respecting male pop star wore makeup, high heels and frocks.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Austin says she always had her suspicions, though. She makes her young self sound rather prudish. "We were walking down Kensington High Street one day, maybe a month after we met, and he pointed to an ex-girlfriend across the road and said, 'Ah, that's my ex-girlfriend, she's bisexual.'" He stopped to chat to her. "And by the time he'd looked back for me, I'd gone. I just thought, mmm, I don't know what that is, but it's not for me." You sound horrified, I say. No, she says, that's not right. "I was intimidated by it. He apologised later. In all those 20 years I knew him, it was probably the only time he apologised."
PAUSE 500
SAY   What did he have to apologise for? "I don't know. I said, I think this is maybe not for me." Even now, she's not sure why she reacted like that. "Retrospectively, I can say he could have been on a fishing trip." You thought he was testing to see whether you were bisexual too? She nods. "But I didn't want a menage a trois. I'm heterosexual. I don't stretch that far, you know."
PAUSE 500
SAY   But she adored being with him. Austin had only had two short-lived relationships before Mercury. "He was different from the others. He was more creative. He was just more interesting. He was always thinking about going to see things. Not just bands. Like he'd go to see the film Cabaret over and over again. I loved listening to him explain why he enjoyed it so much. I enjoyed the journey around his mind. It was like reading a book. You were always on this journey with him."
PAUSE 500
SAY   They moved in together in 1971, and were soon engaged. Mercury was desperate for success, which didn't come quickly. "I remember him saying, 'I am very mindful I am getting older to be a successful musician.'" He was 26 when Queen signed a major recording contract with EMI, and their second single, Seven Seas of Rhye, was a Top 10 hit. This was followed by Killer Queen, which reached No 2.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Austin says their relationship began to change. Mercury's bravado was replaced by a genuine confidence, and he was becoming focused. Austin found she was servicing his needs more. "He'd suddenly say, 'I need a piano. Where can I get a piano?'. I'd say, 'I don't know, but I'll try to find out.' So, of course, I find the piano." It was an upright the owners were getting rid of.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Did the piano change him? "Yes, there was much more purposeful energy. He would sit at the piano, play the odd key, hum a bit and he looked very happy. Transported." By now they were spending less time together. When she was working at Biba, he would often be sleeping. When she returned home, he would be preparing for a gig.
PAUSE 500
SAY   They'd been living together for four years when Mercury said he had something to tell her. He struggled to get the words out. He said, 'I think I'm bisexual.' I said 'No, Freddie, I think you're gay.'" He didn't argue. Was that a huge upset? "No, it was a huge relief, because I wasn't sure whether we were travelling in the same direction any more. Some time earlier I saw a dress and thought, that would be a really nice wedding dress. I asked Freddie, is it worth me buying it, and he looked at me and shook his head. So I knew from there on."
PAUSE 500
SAY   But this is what made their relationship so special, says Austin. She had been devastated by the uncertainty, and now she could reconcile herself to a different life. "We became better friends and it became a better relationship. I was six-and-a-half stone. I was really stressed. And suddenly I'm not stressed any more." For 18 months, they continued living together. By now, Mercury was open with her about the men in his life. His first long-term partner was the show business manager David Minns, she says. "No, we won't go down that road. We're talking chaos here."
PAUSE 500
SAY   Austin became Mercury's PA, and Mercury appeared to think her duties included refereeing his tempestuous relationship with Minns. "There were arguments and fights and I learned to stay outside because I was aware of how chaotic the relationship could be. The longer it went on, the more distance I created because it was burnout time." For you? "Yes, I mean, there's PA-ing and PA-ing!"
PAUSE 500
SAY   What was life like as Mercury's PA? "I became his girl Friday. Miss Reliable. His workhorse." Did she resent that? "I'm not big on the ego. No, I didn't resent it, because I'm very practical. I knew that if I didn't work for him I'd have to work somewhere for someone." Did she begin to feel more like an employee than a friend? "No, but if he had treated me as an employee I would have sucked it up, because I'd know it's because he's in a foul mood or annoyed with someone or maybe I'd done something and not realised. We'd be honest with each other. He once told me I was inefficient, and I totally agreed." What's the worst thing he said to her? "You're inefficient."
