SAY   Queen and Mercury became a phenomenon. In 1975, Bohemian Rhapsody was released. Singles were usually three minutes long. This was a six-minute operetta with a nonsense story, and random references to Galileo, Figaro and Scaramouche. Queen were told by their management it was a suicide single. Bohemian Rhapsody was No 1 for nine weeks and became the biggest-selling single of the 1970s. The band were as unpredictable as they were successful, segueing from classic pop (Don't Stop Me Now) to prog rock (Innuendo), heavy rock (Seven Seas of Rhye), opera (Bohemian Rhapsody), seaside ditties (Seaside Rendezvous), rockabilly (Crazy Little Thing Called Love), synth pop (Radio Gaga), disco and hiphop (Another One Bites the Dust).
PAUSE 500
SAY   Mercury had an astonishing four-octave vocal range, from baritone up to falsetto. Billboard voted him the third greatest rock lead singer of all time (behind only Mick Jagger and Stevie Nicks); ChartMasters ranks Queen as the second bestselling band of all time, behind only the Beatles. In 1985, when their popularity appeared to be waning, they wiped the floor with every other act at Live Aid. And, if anything, their popularity has increased since Mercury's death. The 2018 film Bohemian Rhapsody earned almost $1bn at the box office, the highest grossing music biopic in history.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Did fame change Mercury? "It changed the shape of him," says Austin, "but not his core. You can't get on the bus any more, for example. The shape changes externally, but inside you remain the same. You have to. You don't survive this business if you don't, because your ego will just take over and annihilate you."
PAUSE 500
SAY   Was he ever in danger of his ego taking over? "I saw glimmers of it." When? She says it started when the band was being managed by John Reid. "They were travelling, they were successful and there were certain interesting conversations you overhear. 'Why are we playing in a certain territory?' I won't say where, it's so disrespectful." Mercury thought certain countries were too small for Queen. "That was interesting to me."
