SAY   Mercury was fantastically creative in his final years. There was his 1988 operatic collaboration with Montserrat Caballé, Barcelona; Innuendo, the final Queen album released in his lifetime, in 1991; and the posthumous album, Made in Heaven, in 1995. Mercury was living with Hutton at Garden Lodge in Kensington, a house originally built for the painter Cecil Rea and his wife, the sculptor Constance Halford. He adored the house and its Japanese-style gardens. Austin lived less than a mile away, and she had made sure Garden Lodge was just how he wanted it.
